help() {
	cat <<"HELP";
NAME

	rrssh - remote recursive ssh

SYNOPSIS

	rrssh [<options>...] <main-command> [--- <rrssh commands>...]

DESCRIPTION

	An ansible-like automation tool that uses ssh to jump through any
	number of hosts to run commands and transfer files and directories
	along the way.  Its commands are a basic non-turing complete
	shell-like scripting language provided in arguments or in a file.
	A strong panic mecanism will stop the execution at any unexpected
	failures, either external commands exiting with an unhandled non-0
	code or an internal rrssh failure.

	Rrssh is a bash script that implements a recursive scripting
	language.  Rrssh is, in most part, a bash "kernel" that duplicates
	itself locally or through an ssh connection.  Because of its
	nature, rrssh has an extensive test suite to ensure its
	reliability.

	The only requirements for the target hosts are an old bash version
	and basic utilities.

MAIN COMMANDS

	For the main commands that need a script, the script must be
	provided either with the option --- or -f.

	run

		Run the script.

	check

		Examine the script for syntax errors and other problems
		without running it.

	print [-n <index>] [-lt]

		Prints the script, one token per line.  With -n, only print
		that token.  Tokens are indexed at 0.  With -t, only print the
		amount of tokens.  With -l, do not quote the tokens using
		printf %q.

	print-kernel

		Duplicates the kernel to stdout.  Send it to shellcheck for
		analysis.

OPTIONS

	-h, --help

		Show this help message.

	--- <commands>...

		Stop reading options and arguments.  The remaining arguments
		will be interpreted as a script.  --- is necessary if the
		script is provided by arguments.

	-f, --file <filepath>

		Provide a file of commands to execute instead of reading from
		the arguments.  If file is "-", then read from stdin.

	-p

		Make any command chain that does not exit with 0 trigger a
		rrssh panic.  Default behavior is to only make the parent
		scope exit non-0.

	-i

		Any command host or su that fails will be handled as if their
		internal scope had exited non-0 instead of triggering a panic.

	-n <index>

		Read the description of the command "print".

	-l, -t

		Read the description of the command "print".

EXECUTION

	A rrssh script is usually made of scopes [ ].  Unlike normal
	shells, by default, any command "chain" that exits non-0 will stop
	further execution of the scope and the scope will exit with non-0.
	The -p option will make rrssh panic instead.  By default, any host
	or su command that fails will trigger a panic unless the option -i
	is used.  With -i, a failing host or su command will behave as if
	its inner scope exited non-0.

COMMANDS

	host <destination> [ <commands>... ]
	host <destination> : <commands>...
	sudo-host <destination> [ <commands>... ]
	sudo-host <destination> : <commands>...

		These commands are the main purpose of using rrssh.
		Duplicates the rrssh kernel through a single ssh connection
		and execute the sub commands on the remote host.  This command
		can include itself arbitrarily many times to reach any depth
		in a network.  The [ ] variants are scoped.  The : variants
		take all the commands until the end of the current scope.  The
		sudo variants will run the actual ssh command through sudo.

	su <user> [ <commands>... ]
	su <user> : <commands>...
	sudo-su <user> [ <commands>... ]
	sudo-su <user> : <commands>...

		Same as the host command, but switches user on the same
		machine instead.

	[ <commands>... ]

		Create a subscope and run commands inside.  This is the
		equivalent of a subshell in shell scripting.

	command <system-command>
	command [ <argv>... ]
	--- <argv>... ---

		Call an external command.

	true
	false

		Do nothing, with success or failure.

	print <text>

		Print a line of text to stdout.

	cd <path>
	home
	pushd <path>
	popd

		Change your current directory.

	pwd

		Print the current working directory.

	take-file <path> <new-name> <dropdir>
	take-dir <path> <new-name> <dropdir>

		Bring a file or directory to a drop directory on the current
		host, a parent host, or a future child host.  You can provide
		a new name for the file or directory.  You can preserve the
		current name by providing an empty argument as the name ("").

	drop-dir <name> <path>

		Indicate a drop directory (and its name) to receive files and
		directories taken with take-file or take-dir.  The parent
		directory must already exist.

OPERATORS

	The general syntax of a "command chain" is as follows:

		[try] [not] <command> [(and|or) [not] <command>]...

infix operators

	and
	or

		Run the next command of the chain only if the current exit
		code of the chain is a success or failure.  Behaves exactly
		like && and || in shell scripting.

prefix operators

	not <command>

		Inverses the exit code of the following command.  Behaves like
		preceeding a command with a ! in a shell script.

	try

		Ignore the exit code of the following command chain.  Default
		behavior is to panic when a command chain exists non-0.

OPTIONS USED BY RRSSH'S TEST SUITE

	--make-command-fail <id>

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Makes
		a command fail to validate the behavior of the error paths.

	--fake-su

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Make
		the su commands not really attempt to switch user to prevent
		passwords from breaking the test suite.

COMMANDS USED BY RRSSH'S TEST SUITE

	make-command-fail <id>

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Makes
		a command fail to validate the behavior of the error paths.
		This will be in effect only for the current scope and all
		child scopes.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES USED FOR DEVELOPMENT

	RRSSH_COVERAGE_FILE

		If defined, file path to append code coverage ids encountered.

EXAMPLES

	TODO

PROBLEMS

	TODO

HELP
}

